WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.190 --> 00:00:03.590
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.590 --> 00:00:08.580
This is another one of those amazing papers
that I am really excited about.

00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:12.870
And the reason for that is that this is in
the intersection of computer graphics and

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:17.900
machine learning, which, as you know, is already
enough to make me happy, but when I've first

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:22.890
seen the quality of the results, I was delighted
to see that it delivered exactly what I was

00:00:22.890 --> 00:00:23.960
hoping for.

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:28.020
Light simulation programs are an important
subfield of computer graphics where we try

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:34.530
to create a photorealistic image of a 3D digital
scene by simulating the path of millions and

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:35.999
millions of light rays.

00:00:35.999 --> 00:00:40.909
First, we start out with a noisy image, and
as we compute more paths, it slowly clears

00:00:40.909 --> 00:00:41.909
up.

00:00:41.909 --> 00:00:45.719
However, it takes a very long time to get
a perfectly clear image, and depending on

00:00:45.719 --> 00:00:49.499
the scene and the algorithm, it can take from
minutes to hours.

00:00:49.499 --> 00:00:54.839
In an earlier work, we had a beautiful but
pathological scene that took weeks to render

00:00:54.839 --> 00:00:59.139
on several machines, if you would like to
hear more about that, the link is available

00:00:59.139 --> 00:01:00.489
in the video description.

00:01:00.489 --> 00:01:05.360
So in order to alleviate this problem, many
noise filtering algorithms surfaced over the

00:01:05.360 --> 00:01:06.360
years.

00:01:06.360 --> 00:01:10.110
The goal of these algorithms is that instead
of computing more and more paths until the

00:01:10.110 --> 00:01:15.650
image clears up, we stop at a noisy image
and try to guess what the final image would

00:01:15.650 --> 00:01:16.650
look like.

00:01:16.650 --> 00:01:21.350
This often happens in the presence of some
additional depth and geometry information,

00:01:21.350 --> 00:01:26.920
additional images that that are often referred
to as feature buffers or auxiliary buffers.

00:01:26.920 --> 00:01:31.310
This information helps the noise filter to
get a better understanding of the scene and

00:01:31.310 --> 00:01:33.450
produce higher quality outputs.

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:37.910
Recently, a few learning-based algorithms
emerged with excellent results.

00:01:37.910 --> 00:01:42.970
Well, excellent would be an understatement
since these can take an extremely noisy image

00:01:42.970 --> 00:01:45.760
that we rendered with one ray per pixel.

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:50.410
This is as noisy as it gets I'm afraid, and
it is absolutely stunning that we can still

00:01:50.410 --> 00:01:52.540
get usable images out of this.

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:57.060
However, these algorithms are not capable
of dealing with sequences of data and are

00:01:57.060 --> 00:02:00.360
condemned to deal with each of these images
in isolation.

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:04.830
They have no understanding of the fact that
we are dealing with an animation.

00:02:04.830 --> 00:02:06.130
What does this mean exactly?

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:11.200
What this means is that the network has no
memory of how it dealt with the previous image,

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:15.720
and if we combine it with the fact that a
trace amount of noise still remains in the

00:02:15.720 --> 00:02:18.569
images, we get a disturbing flickering effect.

00:02:18.569 --> 00:02:22.600
This is because the remainder of the noise
is different from image to image.

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:27.350
This technique uses a Recurrent Neural Network,
which is able to deal with sequences of data,

00:02:27.350 --> 00:02:30.100
for instance, in our case, video.

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:35.430
It remembers how it dealt with the previous
images a few moments ago, and, as a result,

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:39.640
it can adjust and produce outputs that are
temporally stable.

00:02:39.640 --> 00:02:43.860
Computer graphics researchers like to call
this spatiotemporal filtering.

00:02:43.860 --> 00:02:48.340
You can see in this camera panning experiment
how much more smoother this new technique

00:02:48.340 --> 00:02:49.360
is.

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:53.520
Let's try the same footage slowed down and
see if we get a better view of the flickering.

00:02:57.680 --> 00:02:59.200
Yup, all good!

00:02:59.220 --> 00:03:04.220
Recurrent Neural Networks are by no means
easy to train and need quite a few implementation

00:03:04.220 --> 00:03:08.740
details to get it right, so make sure to have
a look at the paper for details.

00:03:08.740 --> 00:03:15.230
Temporally coherent light simulation reconstruction
of noisy images from one sample per pixel.

00:03:15.230 --> 00:03:16.620
And for video.

00:03:16.620 --> 00:03:18.160
This is insanity.

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:22.920
I would go out on a limb and say that in the
very near future, we'll run learning-based

00:03:22.920 --> 00:03:28.209
noise filters that take images that are so
noisy, they don't even have one ray sample

00:03:28.209 --> 00:03:29.230
per pixel.

00:03:29.230 --> 00:03:31.760
Maybe one every other pixel or so.

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:33.790
This is going to be the new milestone.

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:38.080
If someone told me that this would be possible
when I started doing light transport as an

00:03:38.080 --> 00:03:41.750
undergrad student, I wouldn't have believed
a word of it.

00:03:41.750 --> 00:03:46.780
Computer games, VR and all kinds of real-time
applications will be able to get photorealistic

00:03:46.780 --> 00:03:50.120
light simulation graphics in real time.

00:03:50.120 --> 00:03:51.510
And, temporally stable.

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:54.130
I need to take some time to digest this.

00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:58.240
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

